### What is NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException?

When Spring finds multiple beans of the same type at an injection point and cannot determine which one to use, it throws `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`. This happens when there is no `@Primary`, no `@Qualifier`, and the parameter name does not match any bean name.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public interface NotificationService {
    void send(String accountId, String message);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + accountId + ": " + message);
    }
}

@Service
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + accountId + ": " + message);
    }
}

@Service
public class PushNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("Push to " + accountId + ": " + message);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    private final NotificationService notification; // three candidates!

    public TransferService(NotificationService notification) {
        this.notification = notification;
    }
}
```

```text
No qualifying bean of type 'NotificationService' available:
expected single matching bean but found 3:
  emailNotificationService, smsNotificationService, pushNotificationService
```

### How does the resolution order work?

Spring resolves ambiguity in this priority order:

| Priority | Mechanism | Where placed | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 (highest) | `@Qualifier("name")` | Injection point | `@Qualifier("smsNotificationService")` |
| 2 | `@Primary` | Bean definition | `@Primary @Service` |
| 3 | Parameter/field name | Injection point | `NotificationService emailNotificationService` |
| 4 | Single candidate | Automatic | Only one bean of the type exists |

If none of these resolves the ambiguity, Spring throws `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`.

### How do you use @Primary?

`@Primary` goes on the bean definition (the class or `@Bean` method) and tells Spring: "When no qualifier is specified, use this one." It is the default fallback.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Primary // default when no @Qualifier specified
@Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + accountId + ": " + message);
    }
}
```

Now `TransferService` receives the email service automatically because it is marked `@Primary`:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    private final NotificationService notification;

    public TransferService(NotificationService notification) {
        this.notification = notification; // gets EmailNotificationService (Primary)
    }

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // ... transfer logic
        notification.send(to, "Received " + amount + " from " + from);
    }
}
```

```text
Email to SAV2002: Received 10000 from SAV2001
```

### How do you use @Qualifier?

`@Qualifier` goes on the injection point (constructor parameter, field, or setter) and explicitly names which bean to inject. It overrides `@Primary`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class FraudAlertService {
    private final NotificationService notification;

    // Explicitly picks SMS even though email is @Primary
    public FraudAlertService(
            @Qualifier("smsNotificationService") NotificationService notification) {
        this.notification = notification;
    }

    public void alertFraud(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        notification.send(accountId,
            "URGENT: Suspicious transaction of " + amount + " detected on account " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
SMS to SAV2001: URGENT: Suspicious transaction of 75000 detected on account SAV2001
```

### How does @Primary differ from @Qualifier?

| Aspect | @Primary | @Qualifier |
|---|---|---|
| Placed on | Bean definition (class or @Bean method) | Injection point (parameter, field, setter) |
| Meaning | "I am the default" | "I want this specific one" |
| Priority | Lower | Higher (overrides @Primary) |
| Count | Only one per type (multiple = error) | Any number per type |
| Use case | Most consumers want the same bean | Specific consumer needs a different bean |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

// @Primary on the bean definition
@Primary
@Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email: " + message);
    }
}

// Consumer with NO qualifier: gets @Primary (email)
@Service
public class TransferService {
    public TransferService(NotificationService notification) {
        // notification = EmailNotificationService
    }
}

// Consumer WITH qualifier: gets SMS (overrides @Primary)
@Service
public class FraudAlertService {
    public FraudAlertService(
            @Qualifier("smsNotificationService") NotificationService notification) {
        // notification = SmsNotificationService
    }
}
```

### How does List and Map injection work?

`List<T>` injects all beans of type T. `Map<String, T>` maps bean names to instances, enabling runtime selection without if-else chains.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

@Service
public class MarketingBroadcastService {
    private final List<NotificationService> allChannels;

    public MarketingBroadcastService(List<NotificationService> allChannels) {
        this.allChannels = allChannels;
    }

    // Broadcasts to ALL channels
    public void broadcastPromotion(String accountId, String promo) {
        allChannels.forEach(ch -> ch.send(accountId, promo));
    }
}

@Service
public class CustomerPreferenceService {
    private final Map<String, NotificationService> channelMap;

    public CustomerPreferenceService(Map<String, NotificationService> channelMap) {
        this.channelMap = channelMap;
    }

    // Selects channel at runtime based on customer preference
    public void notifyByPreference(String accountId, String message, String preferred) {
        NotificationService channel = channelMap.get(preferred);
        if (channel == null) {
            channel = channelMap.get("emailNotificationService"); // fallback
        }
        channel.send(accountId, message);
    }
}
```

```text
// MarketingBroadcastService.broadcastPromotion("SAV2001", "50% off on locker rental")
Email to SAV2001: 50% off on locker rental
SMS to SAV2001: 50% off on locker rental
Push to SAV2001: 50% off on locker rental

// CustomerPreferenceService.notifyByPreference("SAV2001", "EMI due", "smsNotificationService")
SMS to SAV2001: EMI due
```

The Map injection pattern is powerful for runtime dispatch. Adding a new channel (WhatsApp, for example) requires only creating a new `@Service` class. No existing code changes are needed.

### How do custom qualifier annotations work?

String-based `@Qualifier("name")` is fragile because a typo compiles but fails at runtime. Custom qualifier annotations provide type-safe disambiguation:

```java
package com.bank.qualifier;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Qualifier
public @interface PrimaryChannel {}

@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Qualifier
public @interface UrgentChannel {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.qualifier.PrimaryChannel;
import com.bank.qualifier.UrgentChannel;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@PrimaryChannel
@Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email: " + message);
    }
}

@UrgentChannel
@Service
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    @Override
    public void send(String accountId, String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS: " + message);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.qualifier.UrgentChannel;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class FraudAlertService {
    private final NotificationService notification;

    public FraudAlertService(@UrgentChannel NotificationService notification) {
        this.notification = notification; // type-safe, no string
    }
}
```

A typo in `@UrgentChannel` is a compile error. A typo in `@Qualifier("smsNotificationService")` only fails at runtime.

### What are common mistakes with bean disambiguation?

**WRONG -- forgetting to resolve ambiguity:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class LoanService {
    private final NotificationService notification;

    // WRONG -- three candidates, no qualifier, no primary
    public LoanService(NotificationService notification) {
        this.notification = notification;
    }
}
```

```text
NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: expected single matching bean but found 3
```

**WRONG -- multiple @Primary on the same type:**

```java
@Primary @Service
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService { }

@Primary @Service // WRONG -- two @Primary
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService { }
```

```text
NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: more than one 'primary' bean found
```

**CORRECT -- one @Primary, @Qualifier where needed:**

```java
@Primary @Service // default
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService { }

@Service // no @Primary
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService { }

@Service
public class LoanService {
    public LoanService(NotificationService notification) {
        // gets EmailNotificationService (Primary)
    }
}

@Service
public class FraudAlertService {
    public FraudAlertService(
            @Qualifier("smsNotificationService") NotificationService notification) {
        // gets SmsNotificationService (Qualifier overrides Primary)
    }
}
```

### What about @Profile for environment-specific beans?

`@Profile` activates beans based on the environment. Only beans matching the active profile are registered in the container.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Profile("dev")
    public DataSource h2DataSource() {
        // In-memory H2 for development
        return new org.h2.jdbcx.JdbcDataSource();
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("prod")
    public DataSource postgresDataSource() {
        // PostgreSQL for production
        var ds = new com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource();
        ds.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://db.nbi.com:5432/banking");
        return ds;
    }
}
```

```properties
# application.properties
spring.profiles.active=prod
```

With `@Profile("prod")` active, only `postgresDataSource` is registered. There is no ambiguity because `h2DataSource` does not exist in the container.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **@Qualifier with @Bean methods.** When defining beans with `@Bean`, the method name becomes the bean name. Use `@Qualifier` on the method to give it a different qualifier name.
- **Ordered List injection.** Beans in `List<T>` are ordered by `@Order` annotation or `Ordered` interface. Use this to control processing order in a chain-of-responsibility pattern.
- **ObjectProvider for optional beans.** `ObjectProvider<T>` is lazy and null-safe. Use `provider.getIfAvailable()` when a bean may not exist.
- **Conditional beans.** `@ConditionalOnProperty`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` provide more flexible alternatives to `@Profile` for feature flags and fallback beans.
- **Spring Boot auto-configuration** uses `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` extensively: it defines a default bean, and your custom `@Bean` definition replaces it automatically.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What happens if two beans have `@Primary`? (NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.)
- Can you use `@Qualifier` on a constructor parameter? (Yes, and this is the preferred approach with constructor injection.)
- What is the difference between `@Qualifier` and parameter name matching? (Qualifier is explicit and has higher priority; name matching is implicit and fragile.)
- How do you inject all beans of a type in a specific order? (`List<T>` with `@Order` on each bean.)
- Can `@Profile` and `@Primary` be combined? (Yes: `@Profile("prod") @Primary` marks the bean as default only when the prod profile is active.)
